I have just been through more or less the same hoop myself; I got
a wide variety of responses from readers of the alpha-osf-managers
mailing list, which I summarized back to the list as follows:
--------------------------------------------------------------

Many thanks to all who responded (and so quickly)!

It seems from the variety of responses I got that there may well be more
than one cause (maybe several) of daemons not starting as expected at
boot time - particularly as I was only able to get my sample daemon
(DEC's MH pop daemon) going correctly by one method (no. 1 below).
Some I could not test as I don't have the source code.

So here are all the suggestions I have received to date; some may work
better in other circumstances, I guess (they all work for someone!)

1. In the startup script, in place of "command", put
    at now + 1 minute startup-script
   where startup-script contains the necessary command(s).

2. As above but just "at now ..." (no delay)
   This may work as well as (1), but having got it working...

3. General problem with terminal output? (No specific cure provided)

4. In the startup script, in place of "command", put
    /usr/bin/nohup command

5. Change the daemon command to include a call to setsid(), making the
   process a process group leader.

6. Use a separate "wrapper" program to do the setsid() call and then execvp()
   the daemon program

7. Put the (undocumented) "daemon()" call in the source code.

Credits to:
  Craig I Hagan
  Ofer Gan
  Bede Webster
  Bonfield James
  Steve Rosen
  John Kohl
  Martyn Johnson
  Ray Bellis
with apologies for my very simplified summaries!

Richard
