I received this from a developer on a machine I run. Since 
it has been reported to DEC in the past and still not been 
fixed, I think I'll share it here as well.

--
Here is a test case for the alpha assembler bug in converting constants.  
It was reported for OSF/1 1.x and is still present in OSF/1 3.0.

The C program below prints this:

1.0001550095108276e-04 3903a478 3f1a37ed
1.0820747754938310e-01 3941db71 3fbbb37c
1.1979850985234844e-04 ba4aa4f0 3f1f678a
-1.0817311005210172e-02 e7fbfba6 bf862762
-1.5304564931392372e+01 ef47ad6d c02e9bef

All the outputs are incorrect in the bottom bit, even though all five
decimal inputs are very close to exact double precision values.  There
should be no difficulty about which way they should round.  This large
an error in converting from decimal to binary representation violates
the IEEE 754 spec.

The program was compiled with standard cc, version 3.11 with no 
optimizations or other switches to speak of. The bug does not appear when 
the 'cc -migrate' compiler is used.

The right answers are:

1.0001550095108278e-04 3903a479 3f1a37ed
1.0820747754938312e-01 3941db72 3fbbb37c
1.1979850985234846e-04 ba4aa4f1 3f1f678a
-1.0817311005210174e-02 e7fbfba7 bf862762
-1.5304564931392374e+01 ef47ad6e c02e9bef

