I am porting unix code to a Dec Alpha 3000/800 (OFS1 V3.0 347 alpha).
(The unix code currently runs on HP 9000/720 (HP-UX 9.03) and SunOS 4.1.3.)

The code is a mix of C++ and C routines which are compiled with
DEC's cxx and cc, respectively.

The C code contains two routines which use variable argument lists.
I have discovered that after calling one of these routines from
a CXX routine, the last named parameter is garbage.  The same thing
happens if the C, variable arg list, routine is converted to C++.

Barring typos, here is sample code which demonstrates the problem:

--------------------- cut here and save to test.cxx -------

extern "C" {
int viewparms ( char *p1, char *p2, ... );
}

main()
{
    char *p1 = "I am p1";
    char *p2 = "{";
    char *p3 = "}";
    char *p4 = ";";

    viewparms( p1, p2, p3, p4 );

    return(0);
}

------------------------------- end of test.cxx -----------

--------------------- cut here and save to var.c ----------

#include <stdarg.h>

/* VARARGS */
int viewparms( char *p1, char *p2, ... )
{
    va_list  ap;
    char    *p3;
    char    *p4;

    va_start( ap, p2 );

    p3 = va_arg( ap, char * );
    p4 = va_arg( ap, char * );

    va_end( pa );

    return( 0 );
}

------------------------------- end of var.c --------------

--------------------- cut here and save to makefile -------

test:  test.cxx var.o
       cxx -O0 -g1 -o test test.cxx var.o

var.o: var.c
       cc -O0 -g1 -c var.c

----------------------------- end of makefile -------------

Instructions:

1.   Execute:  make test

2.   Run "test" under a debugger (I used DECladebug).

3.   "step" to the viewparms call in main and
     note that all 4 parms are reasonable values.

4.   "step" into viewparms and note that at least p2 is garbage.
     p1 may point to a blank string, too.

Comments:

I have played with the compiler options (various -O and -g values),
changed var.c to var.cxx (then compiling with cxx, of course),
changed "stdarg.h" to "varargs.h" (although The Annotated C++
Reference Manual says not to), tried c89 instead of cc, and so on.

The relevent section of The A.R.M. is Section 8.3, page 146.

Question:

Have I done something wrong or is this just a DEC C++ bug?
Any work-around other than eliminating all variable argument calls?

Thanks for any help,

