I have a few questions relating to C2 Security on OSF/1 V2.0.  What
I am asking may be covered in the Enhanced Security manual, but I
don't have that.  (the man pages aren't as helpful as they could
be -- they just refere you to that manual).

The only reason I want to use C2 is to have shaddowed passwords.
I don't really need the other features of C2.

When I set up a user account, I would like to take their encrypted
password entry from our Sun network, and have OSF use it as an
initial password.  Here lies the problem:  If a user has a password
which is longer than 8 characters on the sun network, the password
is truncated to 8 characters before it is in encrypted.  Since C2
supports longer passwords, there is an inconsistancy.  When the
user tries to log in, they will be unsuccesful because their 9+ 
character password doesn't match the 8 character one which has
been encrypted.  If the user types only the first 8 letters of
their password, there is no problem.

Is there any way I can turn off this "feature"?  It's not that
important to have the capability for more than 8 characters in
the password.  (but I definately want to keep shaddowed passwords).

My second question:  is there a crypt() function I can reference
from a C program which will encrypt a key in the same fassion
that it is encrypted in the auth files (ie. supporting more
than 8 characters?)

I would appreciate any help I can get.  I'm new at OSF/1, and
have partial doccumentation.

