Tim Llewellyn <Tim.Llewellyn@bristol.ac.uk> wrote:
> I've been having fun recently with rc3.d and init.d, for httpd.

I run httpd from inetd.conf, myself.

Of course that makes each connection a little slower, but if you're not
doing a lot of www service (we're behind a firewall, so we aren't) then
you don't need to run it as a daemon.

You should be able to run it from rc3.d/S99*, if it backgrounds itself
properly. I personally don't like the tendency of BSD software to background
itself, and tend to run through the old double-fork-and-setpgrp myself
in a wrapper (I call it detach). Once you do that it doesn't matter if
you're doing it from a script or what.

> What I have done is also add a line to the /etc/inittab file to
> start the daemon.

That will work, but then you need to set the environment right yourself.
In general you should start stuff from inittab that runs to completion,
not daemons... the main point of inittab is that it lets init manage
a bevy of scripts and sessions.
