Greetings!

 I have a question about the implementation of FILE* pointers in OSF1:

I'm trying to port a file server program (the "iserver" protocol, for
Transputer processors, for those who know) to a DEC Alpha running 
OSF1 v.2.1.  Part of the protocol involves a remote device requesting the
server to open a file.  The server does this, and returns a FILE*
pointer (fp).  Whenever the remote device subsequently wants to access
the file, it passes the server its fp.  Unfortunately, the original
protocol design lacked foresight, and hard-coded FILE* pointers as being
32-bits long!! This means that only the lower 4 bytes of the OSF1
fp will get sent to the remote device.  I don't have access to the
code running on the remote device, and hence can't modify the protocol
to support n-bit fp's.  I think this limits my options to a bad hack:
through much poking around, I've discovered that all fp's (that i've
seen so far, anyway) have only 0x3FF in the upper 4 bytes.  Thus, if I
append this value to all fp's received from the remote device, I can
reconstruct the correct pointer.

 My question, then: is this a valid method?  A longer version of the
question is: how exactly are fp's implemented in OSF1, and what is the
significance of moving from 32-bit to 64-bit pointers for FILE
structures?  Can I have it guaranteed that the OS will place the
pointers in known (constant) locations?  (my guess is that since a FILE* 
pointer is associated to a specific stream, the answer to the last question
would be "yes", but i need this confirmed)

i appreciate any help (as soon as possible, please),

thanks,


jan.

