|>
|>If I read this right, you are trying to display a VMS X application to
|>you Alpha OSF/1 box. You find that connections are refused. You find
|>that the same application displays without failure on you NCD terminal.
|>I am assuming that you are using TCP/IP (and not decnet) for these 
|>connections.
|>
|>If I have summarized correctly, then I have a simple explanation for
|>your problem.

I think it is pretty clear that you have misunderstood the problem
(ie got it the wrong way round).

$ set flame/burn=mild
Was it seeing the red rag word VMS that caused your brain to
(I assume temporarily) turn itself off?
$ set flame/off

Anyway, it seems to ME that Mike is logged into Motif on a
Vaxstation, and telnets to the OSF/1 box where he issues the
'setenv DISPLAY mybox:0' command to direct the display back to
the VAXStation, which is refusing the connection. If he was doing
what you interpreted he would be using the VMS 
$ set display/node=myosf/transport=tcpip command surely?

Anyway, if I have diagnosed the problem correctly, there are
a couple of things the check. First, check you have a tcpip 
package installed on your VMS system. This appears to be so 
as Mike says he telnet's between the boxes.

Secondly, check (or ask your VMS system manager to check) that TCPIP 
transport is enabled for the Xserver running on the VAXSTation. This
is a pretty common gotcha with VMS Workstations and IP transport.
What you do is look for the file sys$manager:decw$private_server_setup.com. 
If this does not exist, copy the template 

sys$manager:decw$private_server_setup.template

to the .com, then edit it, and look for the string decw$server_transports.
Change the definition of this symbol to include the string 
TCPIP (it is a comma separated list). Then restart the xserver on your 
VAXStation  ($@sys$manager:decw$startup restart) or reboot it. 

The third possible problem is with security settings. Given that Dave
says he checked these its unlikely to be the problem. STill,
you could try user * node * transport * in the VMS customize 
security, though this is rarely necessary except when desparate
and in a hurry. If you have a badly configured IP network you may
need to use the IP numeric address there.


|>
|>OSF/1 (and most Unix based X11R5 solutions) have security enabled. Most
|>X terminal vendors support this security, but do not enable it by default,
|>but instead expect Xdm do do it for them. As most VMS applications do
|>not understand XDM-AUTHORIZATION-1 or MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1, you will need to 
|>use host based access controll. Look at the manual page for host(1).
|>You will need to issue the command:
|>
|>	xhost + vms_host_name.

This is equivalent I take it to useing the session manager customize
security menu?

Yes, VMS is non-standard (and better) because it also allows one to 
specify individual USERS on particular machines who can access the
server (this only workks between VMS machines unfortunately I believe).

And I still think you interpreted the problem wrong.


