In article <DUHA.94Nov21095051@ralph.biomed.mcgill.ca>,
Jan A. Duha <duha@ralph.biomed.mcgill.ca> wrote:
> I'm trying to port a file server program (the "iserver" protocol, for
> Transputer processors, for those who know) to a DEC Alpha running 
> OSF1 v.2.1.  Part of the protocol involves a remote device requesting the
> server to open a file.  The server does this, and returns a FILE*
> pointer (fp).  Whenever the remote device subsequently wants to access
> the file, it passes the server its fp.

ICK! Treating the local address of dynamically allocated memory as a token
is pretty evil.

Well, since the client only uses this as an open file token, add a level of
indirection. Create a table of open files in the server, and stick the file
pointer in there and return the index.

[use the low 32 bits as the token]
>  My question, then: is this a valid method?

There's no guarantee that the top 32 bits will always be 0x3FF. In fact, it's
likely to change.

> A longer version of the
> question is: how exactly are fp's implemented in OSF1,

You don't want to know. Seriously... a file pointer should be treated as an
opaque token. You shouldn't know about its structure nor how it's allocated.

> and what is the
> significance of moving from 32-bit to 64-bit pointers for FILE
> structures?

Same as going from 32-bit to 64-bit pointers in any other context.

> Can I have it guaranteed that the OS will place the
> pointers in known (constant) locations?

No. It's reasonable to *assume* that a straightforward implementation will
use a static table, since that's what V7 did, but it's unreasonable to
expect that. There are implementations of stdio that don't have an _iob
array (the usual name of this array) anywhere.

> i appreciate any help (as soon as possible, please),

See above. Build your own table and pass an index into it. I wouldn't for
a minute consider any other solution. If you can, get this change incorporated
in the original version of the server. This is a really nasty design.
