In article <3aogri$rn8@nyx10.cs.du.edu>, mhatz@nyx10.cs.du.edu (Mike Hatz) writes:
|> 
|> I have a Dec Alpha running osf/1 3.0.  I sit down and log into a vms
|> workstation.  In the decterm window I telnet back to my box.  I use
|> 'setenv DISPLAY mybox:0'.  Then when I try to run ANY xbased display on
|> the vms machines,  I get a "can't open display xtoolkit error".  This
|> does not occur when I use the NCDs running plain old lat.  The vms
|> machines are running VMS-6.2-FT with multinet.  I checked security
|> settings on both machines and that does not seem to be the problem.  I
|> would really like the vms machines to be able to access some of my
|> x-based servers and would appreciate any help/hints.  Thanks.
|> 
|> Mike
|> 

If I read this right, you are trying to display a VMS X application to
you Alpha OSF/1 box. You find that connections are refused. You find
that the same application displays without failure on you NCD terminal.
I am assuming that you are using TCP/IP (and not decnet) for these 
connections.

If I have summarized correctly, then I have a simple explanation for
your problem.

OSF/1 (and most Unix based X11R5 solutions) have security enabled. Most
X terminal vendors support this security, but do not enable it by default,
but instead expect Xdm do do it for them. As most VMS applications do
not understand XDM-AUTHORIZATION-1 or MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1, you will need to 
use host based access controll. Look at the manual page for host(1).
You will need to issue the command:

	xhost + vms_host_name.

/Dave


