We have observed that the file permissions on files in the /usr/man/cat1
directory are created as -rw-rw-rw- thus allowing any user to modify 
their contents without a trace to who did it.  The following is a sample:

-rw-rw-rw-   1 root     bin        23860 Sep 24 15:57 yacc.1
-rw-rw-rw-   1 keppingm system       776 Nov 17 10:58 yes.1
-rw-rw-rw-   1 root     bin         1420 Nov  9 09:18 ypcat.1
-rw-rw-rw-   1 root     bin         3513 Nov  9 09:18 ypmatch.1
-rw-rw-rw-   1 barrerat bin         1916 Nov  4 11:38 yppasswd.1
 
We are having the problem on an AXP 2100 running OSF/1 Version 2.1B.  We
did not have this problem on our DEC 5900 running Ultrix 4.3 which would
have the following permissions (at least we would know who modified its
contents):

-r--r--r--  1 crowede      3619 Sep 15  1993 filter.1
-r--r--r--  1 crowede      2227 Jun 25  1992 fortran.1
-r--r--r--  1 crowede       255 Jun  4  1992 gams.1
-r--r--r--  1 crowede       364 Jun  4  1992 gaussian.1
-r--r--r--  1 root         5003 Nov 15 13:33 grep.1

This was reported to DEC Support and the conclusion was that this is the
way it should work on OSF/1.  I would say it is a bug.  Anyone else that
feels that this is a bug also, please call it in.  

As an interim workaround we will create a cron job to change the permissions.


