Hi,

in a large inhouse project we've done the porting in the opposite
direction (from DEC C++ OSF/1 to HPUX) and discovered the following
problems:
the HP C++ Compiler is much stricter in the interpretation of the ARM,
but going zhe other direction should be a snap.
As long as You make no assumptions about the size and alignment of
data types (you pointed out out some examples already) there should be
no problems there.
The major difference is the instantiation of templates.
On DEC C++ you have to define a pragma macro for each and every
template signature you intend to use:

  Example: #pragma templateClass <CONTAINER>;

You must include the source file for your container class and compile
the whole thing into one object module (this can take a while).

Some other differences are the number of libraries to include in the
makefile, but that should be relatively straight forward...

Good luck, Martin

BtW, my mail address at the office is Bodenstedt@suo.mts.dec.com
  (guess who I work for...)


