Peter da Silva <peter@nmti.com> wrote:
}Peter Lister <p.lister@cranfield.ac.uk> wrote:
}> I tarred the directory and gzip managed 94% compression on the tar
}> file (yes, that's right, reduced the size by 94%), so these files are
}> obviously pretty sparse. It would seem that I-DEAS can take advantage
}> of some pretty good file compression in the OSF1 filesystem when it
}> writes it's files, but tar,cp,etc don't manage this.

}To be precise... if you seek to a point beyond the end of a file then write,
}blocks between the end of the file and the new seek point will not be
}allocated until they are written to, and will read as blocks of zeros.
}
}A database program will tend to do this, producting what's called a "sparse
}file".
}
}If you write a block of zeros, it will be allocated, so tar and cp and so on
}will populate a sparse file.
}
}The fix is to have a program that seeks (on output) over blocks of zeroes (on
}input) as it writes. It's not a complex program...

   Or, just use "dd conv=sparse ..."

John
