: install a campus wide NFS file server that will initially
: support 10,000 users.  We'd like to run DEC's Advanced File
: System so that recovery from system crashes would be fairly
: fast.   


We have begun phasing out our Ultrix based fileservers onto
a pair of AXP3000/500's running OSF/1 3.0. We export AdvFS
volumes simultaneously into DCE/DFS (AFS4) and NFS. We have
tried to simulate a hard-crash and have found that the time
it takes for file-system recovery to take under 1 minute.
We have 4 RZ74S drives (DEC DSP5300S) - two on the internal
bus, two on the external SCSI bus.

In this configuration, the most significant delays in
crash recovery are attributed to DFS token state recovery.
It takes upwards of 5 minutes for filesets to become available
after a simulated crash so that (1) the bosserver's can
elect a new co-ordinator and (2) the filset servers can
re-establish file state with DFS clients that hold tokens
on outstanding file transactions.  Because we dual export,
we do NOT fire up the NFS server until the DFS fileservers
have re-established token state. This behavior is to be
expected in AFS/DFS based implementations, if you run
pure NFS, you can expect to see under 2 minute downtime
between server crash and re-start using AdvFS. The advantages
of using AFS/DFS far outweigh this minor time factor.


I hope to do further benchmarking between AdvFS and UFS as
network filesystem backing store.. The most interesting
performance metric for us to look at is write latency over
NFS between using AdvFS vs UFS


-- Jim Doyle

