Summary: How to resolve 'out of swap space' problems
Keywords: swap space, xwindows, xdm
X-Newsreader: TIN [version 1.2 PL0]

****  TRY THIS BEFORE MESSING WITH SWAP SETTINGS *****

On our Alpha OSF/1 system, we had the message about swap space
after 2-4 weeks of use.  We poked around, looked at man pages,
tried various things, would reboot, and things would be OK for
another 2-4 weeks.

Finally, after careful reading of the xdm man page, I found what I
needed:


In the file /usr/lib/X11/xdm/xdm-config, add the following line
(anywhere in the file):

DisplayManager._0.terminateServer: true

You'll probably need to restart the system at this point, but may
be able to get away with exiting the Display Manager (i.e. get back
to the login window).  This setting causes xdm to COMPLETELY quit
and start over, rather than hang on to all that memory that was
allocated.  This takes about 15 seconds.  It seems that X is pretty
bad about releasing memory, and if you monitor the size of the xdm
process, you'l just see it grow and grow and grow.  I hope this
helps -- it worked for me!!

-- Jeff Brush
jeffyb@wam.umd.edu

(feel free to say 'thanks').

A bird in the hand still needs to be plucked.




