Sorry if this is a newbie question, but the FAQ doesn't seem to touch it.

I moved a user from one file system to another (both ufs, not advfs or afs). This guy has several large files in his home dir generated by SDRC I-DEAS, a big CAD/CAM package. The amount of space occupied by the copied directory hierarchy occupied something like 50% more space as reported by du. On further inspection, individual I-DEAS data files all took up about 50-100% more space in the copy than the original. I tried test copies with cp, tar and dd. All took up the same increased amount of space according to du. The copies were the identical length according to ls -l and had identical checksums according to sum.

I tarred the directory and gzip managed 94% compression on the tar file (yes, that's right, reduced the size by 94%), so these files are obviously pretty sparse. It would seem that I-DEAS can take advantage of some pretty good file compression in the OSF1 filesystem when it writes it's files, but tar,cp,etc don't manage this. My guess is that I-DEAS uses mmap(3) and that that virtual memory paging writes the file blocks more efficiently than stream I/O as used by cp, tar, etc.

Anyone care to comment? The obvious next question is to ask whether there is a way to get the copies to "compress" empty/sparse blocks like this.

