I've had a few replies to my query from earlier today re chroot(1).  None
of them were completely on-target, but one was close (see below).  I had
one suggestion that /etc/svc.conf was needed in the chrooted directory,
but by the time that suggestion arrived, I'd already had a look at that
possibility (a sensible one, since svc.conf has a "passwd=" entry, so who
knows what might happen if svc.conf were missing?), but it turns out that
it isn't needed.  (One can actually infer this without checking, by doing
an "ls -l" (from a non-chrooted environment, of courrse), and then doing a
"ls -lu /etc/svc.conf", and noticing that its atime doesn't change.)

The best suggestion came from Joseph McGuire (joe@alpha.vyne.com), who said

   I beleive that you need to create the (dir and) file
   CHROOTDIR/bin/sia/siainitgood.  [ ... ]

Actually, he meant /etc/sia/siainitgood.  As it turns out, that's not quite
enough (it's necessary but not sufficient).  What you *really* need in the
chrooted environment for "ls" to map numeric uids and gids to user and group
names is the following:

	etc/group
	etc/passwd.dir
	etc/passwd.pag
	etc/sia/bsd_matrix.conf
	etc/sia/matrix.conf
	etc/sia/siainitgood

I had the first three; Joe suggestd the last.  But you also need those .conf
files in the etc/sia directory (matrix.conf is a symlink to bsd_matrix.conf).

By the way, in case anyone thinks that this is a pretty trivial problem to be
worrying about, this isn't actually what prompted the original posting in the
first place.  The *real* problem is that we have an application which we want
to run chroot'ed, and it couldn't run because calls to getpwuid() failed, and
in the course of investigating *that* problem, I discovered that even things
like the "ls" example failed.

Anyway, all is now working happily.  Thanks for the suggestions, everyone!

