I'm following up on my own posting, because now I know WHAT is going
on, and it seems to me like the problem lies in the way things are
defined in the standard include files.

Here's the poop:  The variable i->ioc_cmd is of type int, so gets
loaded into a 64-bit register with sign extension.  The highest bit
is set in my example, so the high longword gets filled with ones. All 
of the ioctl codes defined with _IOW(..) are of the form 
(0x80000000 | .. | sizeof(.)), and the sizeof() operator makes this
whole constant expression actually to be of type long (maybe unsigned
long, I'm not sure.... but it is an 8 byte data type).  Thus when
this value is used in a 64-bit register, it is NOT sign extended,
and the high longword is zero.  Thus the equality test will always
be false.

Now ALL "write ioctl's" have the high bit set, and the ioctl codes
are defined through _IOW(..).  Since this is the case, it doesn't
seem like an equality test will EVER succeed between an ioc_cmd
and an ioctl code (constant).  Isn't this a fairly severe error
in the include file definitions?  Shouldn't, for example, _IOW(..)
(or _IOC(..)) include a cast to type int?  I am fixing my code
by casting ever single ioctl constant code to an int, but that
shouldn't really be unnecessary....

