>sacrificing performance where in the extreme; 9 1G partions re-glued to
>gether with volumn manager would deliver higher performance??

Some of the performance problems of older UNIX systems came from
fact that the superblock and inodes were at the beginning of the
partition and data afterward.  Also the seek time of disks was
much slower then.

Today, a lot of vendors are using the Berkeley Fast File System
and the data and inodes are kept close together.  And that 9 GB
disk being discussed is a wee-bit faster than older disks.

Assuming your 9 1 GB partitions are still on the same disk will
it really have better performance than the single 9 GB disk?
Are you organizing the 9 partitions to better fit the I/O load?

