I'm using a 3000/300 v3.0 OSF/1 and the DEC 4mm DAT drive that came 
in the storage works bay to back up 3.6Gb of local disk, 2Gb of NFS
mounted partitions.

In short I have come to believe that the mt driver for the DAT 
device is weak, and prone to problems;

1) mt -f /dev/rmt0h rewind
works some times. 

2) mt -f /dev/rmt0h offline 
then push the tape back in and set to online begets an error.

3) Do enough of the above steps and pax </dev/rmt0h will always report
an error and you have to reboot to clear it.

4) find . -type f -print|pax -w >/dev/rmt0h 
many times will happily write every thing.

pax </dev/rtm0h 
will read back portions of what was written, sometimes with an error
usually without an error.

5) I've totally given up on cpio and dd reading and writting to /dev/rmt**.

Funny that cpio has MORE problems than pax, given that it's the same
executable file (they're all physical links to same file; tar, cpio pax)

So, my use of OSF as a back up station has consummed alot of time and 
caused me to hate DAT drives in general, as I've also heard similar 
stories about DAT on other Unix boxes too.

Any other helps or alternatives??

Thanks.



