Charlie,

   While it doesn't have a full-screen mode, the v3.0 DECladebug debugger, 
available as part of the Developer's Toolkit on the OSF/1 CD, supports C, C++, 
Fortran 77, Fortran 90, Ada, and COBOL.  You can use it just like dbx.
For Fortran, DECladebug:

	- provides access to COMMON blocks
	- supports Fortran 90 derived-type variables
	- understands Fortran RECORD variables
	- understands most Fortran data types
	- does much, much more - but you'll have to see the user's manual
	  for details.

   More Fortran support is in the pipeline for future releases.  (support
   for alternate entry points, for one example.)

   DECladebug (in 3.0) also:

	- accepts the dbx command set
	- supports C, C++, FORTRAN-77, FORTRAN-90, Ada, and COBOL
	- supports debugging threads
	- attaches/detaches to/from running processes
	- comes with EMACS integration support
	- has significant performance improvements over 2.0
	- contains even more

   If you get FUSE, you can also get an integrated Motif GUI for 
DECladebug.  (If you've seen the recent versions of the new and
improved GUI for the VMS Debugger, this will look *very* familiar - like
almost identical.)

p.s. - thanks for the nice words about the VMS Debugger (I worked on it
from 4.7 to 6.1)
