In Article <39t9e8$2ph@info.epfl.ch>
monnier@di.epfl.ch (Stefan Monnier) writes:
>The standard mail configuration on our alphas doesn't know about
>the 8th bit of every byte.
>
>Is sendmail so lame as to only deal with 7bit data (pretty sad
>given the system is fairly new and 8bit isn't rocket science) or is it
>just due to a bad config ?

Welcome to the demented work of network mail. According to the Internet
specification RFC-821, mail is limited to 7 bit data. That's it.

While modern networks all handle octets quite well, but the standards date back
to the days when LOTS of system used 7 bit+parity serial lines and there was no
certainty that an 8 bit message could be handled. Back then many systems even
used 6 bit ASCII, so UUENCODE was developed to allow transmission of 8 bit data
over 6 bit media. MIME generally uses BASE64 encoding for this since it is a 7
bit coding and is far more efficient than UUENCODE.

To get around this limitation (and for many other reasons) MIME was developed.
It allows transfer of any type of data, including 8 bit binary, by encoding it
into 7 bits data.

Sendmail can be configured to handle 8 bit data and any sendmail handling
Extended SMTP can negotiate an 8 bit channel, but it must never be assumed that
8 bits are available. To this day that is often NOT the case.

