> In article <TLB.94Oct30142155@chardonnay.harvard.edu>,
> Trevor Blackwell <tlb@das.harvard.edu> wrote:
> > There are 2 Alphas, running OSF1 2.0 revision 240. 
> > Alpha A mounts some disks on alpha B.
> 
> > When I run mkfifo on alpha A, to the NFS-mounted file system serverd
> > from alpha B, I get:
> 
> NFS does not provide UNIX semantics on remote devices. In particular,
> things like FIFOs can't be implemented due to NFS' stateless semantics.

Well, it works between suns, and between sun and HPUX, and between a
BSDI server and a sun client, and between two BSDI machines, and
between two HPUX machines. (that's what I personally tested on our
network.)

The statelessness of NFS refers to the protocol. The fifo-ness of a
file is a function of its mode bits - the same ones that tell you if
it's readable, etc.

Remember, referencing a FIFO (or any special file) does not do
anything to the server machine. If I write to a console device that is
on a NFS-mounted file system, it writes to the ___client's___ console.
The kernel just sees the mode bits when you open it and says, "Oh, he
wants to write to the console". So you can't NFS-mount another
machine's devices and try to use them over the network that way.

It's clear that it is the OSF1 __server__ implementation that is
broken, because the same problem exists when I try to create such a
file from any client.

