> Bin Li

I changed the creation of hosts.byname and hosts.byaddr in
/var/yp/Makefile the following way:

originally:
$(SED) -e "/^#/d" -e s/#.*$$// $(DIR)/hosts | $(STDHOSTS) \
    | $(AWK) '{for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) print $$i, $$0}' \
    | $(MAKEDBM) - $(YPDBDIR)/$(DOM)/hosts.byname; \
$(STDHOSTS) $(DIR)/hosts | \
    $(AWK) 'BEGIN { OFS="\t"; } $$1 !~ /^#/ { print $$1, $$0 }' \
    | $(MAKEDBM) - $(YPDBDIR)/$(DOM)/hosts.byaddr; \

now:
( $(SED) -e "/^#/d" -e s/#.*$$// $(DIR)/hosts | $(STDHOSTS) \
    | $(AWK) '{for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) print $$i, $$0}' \
    ; echo YP_INTERDOMAIN) | $(MAKEDBM) - $(YPDBDIR)/$(DOM)/hosts.byname; \
( $(STDHOSTS) $(DIR)/hosts | \
    $(AWK) 'BEGIN { OFS="\t"; } $$1 !~ /^#/ { print $$1, $$0 }' \
    ; echo YP_INTERDOMAIN) | $(MAKEDBM) - $(YPDBDIR)/$(DOM)/hosts.byaddr; \

It works on OSF/1 1.3 and 2.0 YP master servers.
To enable the Sun clients to do DNS, I set up a YP slave server on a
Sun and made sure (with ypbind -ypsetme/ypset) the Suns bind to this
slave server.

It doesn't seem to harm other client machines, and as long as they don't
bind to the Sun, they never even know that something is strange.

We'll install Solaris 2.4 when it arrives here; then we won't need this
hack anymore, as Solaris 2.x has the equivalent of /etc/svc.conf:
/etc/nsswitch.conf

