In article <39sn7h$gjl@hermes.uni-konstanz.de>,
Eberhard Heuser-Hofmann <vaxinf@chclu.chemie.uni-konstanz.de> wrote:
> I'm not sure if it's a feature of chown or a bug of OSF/1 V3.0:

It's neither. It's an inevitable result of the file system design.

".*" expands to "all files beginning with "." which expands to ". .. .cshrc
.login ...". Then "chown -r" takes each of these files and recursively chowns
everything it finds.

So it looks at "." and does ". ./* ./*/* ..."

Then it looks at ".." and does "../ ../* ../*/* ..."

By now you've chowned everything from the parent directory to "." onwards.

> Remark: why is there no verbose-option in chown to see what happens?

"find $args -print" will do that...

I generally assume I don't have anything named ".a .b .c ..." and use ".??*".
