In article <39t9e8$2ph@info.epfl.ch>,
Stefan Monnier <monnier@di.epfl.ch> wrote:
>Is sendmail so lame as to only deal with 7bit data (pretty sad
>given the system is fairly new and 8bit isn't rocket science) or is it
>just due to a bad config ?

Welcome to the world of internet mail.  The answer to your question is
"yes and no".  This discussion really belongs on comp.mail.sendmail --
discussions about 8-bit functionality in extensions to SMTP don't
really have anything to do with OSF/1 in particular.

Sendmail is traditionally 7-bit only.  This has nothing to do with being
"lame".  It has to do with standards, and the fact that sendmail was
first implemented according to standards over ten years old.  You
should praise $DIETY that the standards change infrequently or we would
all be in very deep shit.  There are thousands upon thousands of old
SMTP-compliant mailers on the internet that will NEVER become 8-bit
clean.  However, sendmail v8 *does* have some facility for handling
8-bit data.  I believe the primary functionality of this is targeted at
handling character sets other than US-ASCII (maybe this is what you
want?).

This is documented in RFC 1652, on 8-bit MIME support in ESMTP.

Again, just how useful the 8-bit functionality will be in your
environment depends entirely on what the other systems you exchange
mail with can support.  If they are all older 7-bit implementations
without ESMTP, then this will be a waste of time for you, and you
should focus instead on intelligent mail user agents that will
encode prior to delivery.

If you want to read the specification, you can find it on...

ftp.uu.net:/inet/rfc/rfc1652.Z
unix.hensa.ac.uk:/uunet/inet/rfc/rfc1652.Z

If you want to try using sendmail v8, it is on...

ftp.cs.berkeley.edu:/ucb/sendmail/sendmail.8.6.9.*.tar.Z

For readings related to configuring sendmail v8 to handle 8-bit
data, read the sendmail operations guide (in the sendmail "base"
distribution) and the configuration guide (cf/README in the
"cf" distribution).  The book "Sendmail" from O'Reilly publishers
is also recommended.

I run it under OSF/1 2.0 without any problems so far...

--
Paul Southworth
CICNet Systems Support
