In article <1994Nov6.161733@sdg.dra.com>, sean@sdg.dra.com (Sean Donelan) writes:
|>
|>How can one setup an HP Laserjet 4 with a network card as a printer on
|>a DEC OSF/1 without HP's unix software?
|>

Hi,

I suppose you've bought the printer with the same card (JetDirect) as
we did.

To boot and configure the printer you have to do the following:

1) Enable the 'bootp' protocol in the '/etc/inetd.conf' file by uncommenting
   the line.

2) Enable 'tftp' in the same way. Behind the last entry in the line (tftp)
   add (for security) the parameters '-r /boot /boot', where '/boot' is
   the directory which contains the configuration file.
   With these options tftp adds the path /boot before each requested file
   (-r /boot), and allows only to get files out of /boot (second /boot).
   If you have already configured tftp you will know what you have to add.

3) Add one entry for your printer in the '/etc/bootptab' file (described
   in the manual of the JetDirect card):


   hplaser:\
      :hn:ht=ether:vm=rfc1048:\
      :ha=ethernet-address:\
      :ip=internet-address (printer):\
      :lg=internet-address (workstation):\
      :T144="hplaser.cfg":
   
   In the entry above it is assumed that the name of the printer is hplaser
   and the configuration file is named hplaser.cfg

4) Add the file hplaser.cfg in the directory /boot. If the file is empty
   it is allowed to print from all workstations. If you want to restrict
   the access, read the manual.

5) Restart the 'inetd' process.


Now, your printer should work.


Volker

