X-Newsreader: IBM NewsReader/2 v1.00
Date: Mon, 7 Nov 1994 15:02:01 GMT

DECs DECmessageQ (DMQ) is a very useful, albeit vendor specific,
high-level implementation of intra-node communication routines
for sending "message" data between nodes and applications.

If you have used this product, I would like to hear any war stories
you have. I'll share my immediate war-story with you here.

First let me say that DMQ IS cool. Everything works as advertised
UNTIL we started using the Message Recovery System (MRS) option.
Basically, that option routes all message traffic through disk based
queues in order to preserve data in spite of hardware failures. Cool.
At that point we discovered that one major feature, an ability to
perform complex-filtered "gets" from a specific queue failed.

It turns out that MRS does not "mirror" DMQ queues, on disk, it
effectively REPLACES the queues. Messages are eeked-off of the 
disk-queue in fifo order, to the in-memory version of the queue. 
The complex-filtered "get" function only looks at the [in-memory] version
 of the queue, which only has one message at a time actually IN it.
So, surprise, surprise, no complex filtered gets with DMQ for UNIX.

This behavior is not documented. DEC says it was "designed" that way.
It seems that either the coders or the tech writers screwed-up.

Bottom-line, if your application requires recoverability via the message
recovery service of DMQ, watch-out. Behavior with MRS on is not fully
documented.

-Ron Pineda (Burlington Northern Railroad, Electronic RR initiative)



