The malloc() algorithm did change in V2.0 of OSF/1.  Some further enhancements
were added in V3.0, but the algorithmic change was in V2.0.  The new algorithm
packs memory closer together, so invalid applications that write beyond the
end of allocated buffers may tend to break more often with this new malloc().

Do you have V3.0 installed?  If so, there is a tool on your system that may
help you track down the failure.  Install the "ATOM Program Analysis Tools"
subset (subset name ATMBASE300) and you will be able to use the Third Degree
tool.  Third Degree checks your application's memory allocation and usage and
tells you if you do anything wrong like write off the end of an allocated
buffer, etc.

There is a manual for the Third Degree tool in the file:

	/usr/lib/atom/doc/3rd.ps


