Yet another snag in my attempt to get two machines to talk TCP/IP over
modems...

We have two DEC Alpha machines running OSF/1 V3.0, each with a US Robotics
Sportster 28.8 modem connected to it.  We want to be able to run SLIP using
slattach at the highest possible speed that the modems can support.  Both
the 'local' machine and the 'remote' machine are currently here in our office
until we get this up and running.

Here is how we are making things connect up:

1) Have slattach running on the remote machine at all times,
2) Use tip on the local machine to talk to the modem to make it dial,
3) When the modem connection is made, fire up slattach on the local machine.
4) At this point, the SLIP connection is established.

If we configure slattach to run at 9600 baud on both machines AND if we use a
9600 buad configuration for tip to dial up the remote machine, everything
works just fine.  However, if we use ANY other combination for baud rates,
(ignoring slower baud rates, of course), pings of the remote machine fail.
That is, if we just double all the baud rates to 19200, tip successfully
causes the modems to connect at 19200, but the slip connection doesn't seem
to want to work at 19200.  The transmit light on the local modem and the
receive light on the remote modem both flash as each ping packet is sent to
the remote machine, but the return packet never comes back.  Anybody got any
ideas on what we need to do to get this to work at higher speeds?

Furthermore, tip and slattach only seem to consider 9600, 19200, and 38400 as
valid high speed baud rates (14400 and 28800 result in 'unknown baud rate'
messages).  Is there a way to get tip and/or slattach to consider 14400 and
28800 (or any other more-or-less standard high speed baud rate) as valid baud
rates?

Also, how can we take advantage of the compression capabilities of our modems
that should allow us to acheive data rates as high as 115200 baud?  Can the
serial ports on our machines (a 2100 A500MP and a DECpc AXP150) sustain that
kind of transfer rate?  If not, what's the limit of how fast we can go?

We need this connection to be absolutely as fast as possible, given the
hardware that we have available.  Essentially, the remote machine will be
acting as a remote X display of a process running on the local server.  Thus,
we need to be able to get as much throughput as possible so that the X display
is not too doggy.  At 9600 baud, it is clearly unacceptable, so we need to
get this connection running at a MUCH faster speed.

Thanks for any and all help,

Jay


