In <1994Nov1.234121.20371@rad.verbex.com> jtalbott@rad.verbex.com (Jay R. Talbott) writes:

>Furthermore, tip and slattach only seem to consider 9600, 19200, and 38400 as
>valid high speed baud rates (14400 and 28800 result in 'unknown baud rate'
>messages).  Is there a way to get tip and/or slattach to consider 14400 and
>28800 (or any other more-or-less standard high speed baud rate) as valid baud
>rates?
Modem speed indications like 14k4 and 28k8 are not related to the speed a seral
port is communicating with the modem. 
The speed of the line between the modem and serial connection of your system
can communicate is standardized and implemented in the serial chip in your
computer. See /usr/include/termios.h for definitions.
The AXP150 has an 16450 chip ( at least the model I work with ) .
As in PC's this chip cannot work properly above 38400 baud. Higher speeds
causes character loss.

Modem communication speed is determined by your modem settings and the
speed the modems agree upon when two modems negotiate during communication
setup. This may be higher than the speed the modem is talking to the computer
due to the use of compression.
Using 14k4 as modem speed or higher with data compressing may result in 
much higher transfer speed than the line communication speed between the modem
in the computer can handle. 

A general advice is to set hardware handshaking on ( RTS/CTS ) and to use
a serial port that has a 16550 communication chip.

I cannot say anything about setting up slip as I have not used it myself (yet)

